  * 8-bit RGB image of 32 bits a pixel in the surface's own byte order (B, G, R first in memory) with its alpha
  * skipped (or premultiplied when the mode is copy), no mask or decode array, and a transform along the axes that keeps
  * it within two pixels of its own size (rows either way up). O2_NO_IMAGE_FAST_PATH=1 turns it off (A/B). */
+/* (CHONK2, 0373) The same opaque image at another scale along the axes -- a window's picture made in points
+ * shown on a desk at 150 % (ChonkCraft's 1280x778 frame covers 1920x1167 pixels) -- is resampled here: each
+ * device pixel is the bilinear blend of the four source pixels around its centre, as the general path's
+ * resampler gives, but through a table of source columns and weights made once per row length and two
+ * channels at a time in a 32-bit word, each source row resampled across once for the two device rows that
+ * use it. The general path cost that frame about 27 ms (83 % of the main thread; the game ran at 34 frames a
+ * second). Same conditions as the copy above, and also a premultiplied image in the normal blend mode, blended
+ * over the surface (the layer ChonkCraft reads back from GL is one: kCGImageAlphaPremultipliedFirst); the
+ * interpolation none stays on the general path. O2_NO_IMAGE_SCALE_FAST_PATH=1 leaves scaled images to the
+ * general path (A/B). One thread, the drawing thread. */
+static int sfScaleDisabled(void){
+   static int disabled=-1;
+
+   if(disabled<0)
+    disabled=getenv("O2_NO_IMAGE_SCALE_FAST_PATH")!=NULL;
+   return disabled;
+}
